# =============================================================================
# labwc autostart — what runs once labwc is up
# =============================================================================
# labwc runs this file as a shell script after it has read rc.xml and set the
# variables from the `environment` file next to this one. By that point
# WAYLAND_DISPLAY exists, which is why the shell can only be started from here
# and not from ../aquarius-session.
#
# NO PATHS IN THIS FILE. The shell is started with a bare `qs`, which finds its
# configuration through QS_CONFIG_PATH, exported by ../aquarius-session before
# labwc was launched. That is what keeps this file the same on every machine.
#
# The equivalent line in the niri configuration is `spawn-at-startup "qs"`.
# =============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Tell systemd and D-Bus that a screen now exists.
#
# This has to happen BEFORE the target below is started, because the services
# that target pulls in read their environment once, as they start.
#
# The services that make a desktop work — the portals, the keyring, the tray —
# are not started by this file. They are started on demand, later, by systemd's
# user manager and by D-Bus. They do not inherit anything from here; they only
# know what was pushed into those two managers. And ../aquarius-session could
# not push WAYLAND_DISPLAY, because at the moment it ran, labwc had not started
# and there was no Wayland display to name.
#
# So this is the pass that fixes that, from the first point in the session where
# WAYLAND_DISPLAY actually exists. --all sends the whole environment, which also
# covers XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP — the variable xdg-desktop-portal reads to decide
# which backend answers a screen-recording or file-dialog request.
#
# Skipping this is a classic cause of "the file dialog does not open and nothing
# is logged", because a portal that cannot tell which display to talk to fails
# silently rather than loudly. It is also what left /usr/libexec/aquarius-keys-run
# in AquariusOS having to work out the desktop name for itself.
#
# `systemctl --user import-environment` is the same idea for a machine without
# dbus-update-activation-environment, which lives in the dbus-tools package.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if command -v dbus-update-activation-environment > /dev/null 2>&1; then
    dbus-update-activation-environment --systemd --all 2>/dev/null || true
else
    systemctl --user import-environment 2>/dev/null || true
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Tell systemd the graphical session has begun.
#
# Portals, notification daemons and keyring agents are systemd user services
# that say "start me with graphical-session.target". labwc ships
# labwc-session.target, which binds to that target, but labwc does not start it
# itself — starting it is opt-in, and this is the opt-in.
#
# Without this line the portals may never start, and screen recording and file
# dialogs quietly do nothing. niri does the equivalent itself via --session.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
systemctl --user --no-block start labwc-session.target 2>/dev/null || true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. The Aquarius Shell.
#
# WHY THE REDIRECT (2026-09-02). On the first real boot, on niri, the session
# log held nothing but the compositor's own output — the shell had crashed at
# start-up and its error went nowhere, because a compositor hands its children
# /dev/null for stdout and stderr. (Measured on niri 26.04; labwc's autostart is
# a plain shell script and is friendlier than that, but relying on where a
# compositor happens to point its own output is how the first boot was wasted.)
#
# So the shell's output is appended, explicitly, to the SAME log the launcher
# writes, and every line is prefixed so it cannot be confused with the
# compositor's. One file holds the whole story of a boot.
#
# NO PATH HERE EITHER — see the note at the top. $AQ_LOG is exported by
# ../aquarius-session, so this line is identical on every machine. The
# `:-/dev/null` fallback covers starting labwc by hand without the launcher.
#
# If it crashes, labwc keeps running and you keep your windows — you get a
# session with no bar, which is ugly but survivable, and typing `qs` in a
# terminal brings it back. That is deliberately better than the alternative,
# where a broken bar logs you out. (It is also why ../aquarius-session starts
# labwc without its -S/--session flag, which would tie the compositor's life to
# the shell's.)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
qs 2>&1 | sed -u 's/^/[shell] /' >> "${AQ_LOG:-/dev/null}" &

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. The welcome, once, on the first login.
#
# ⚠️ THIS USED TO BE THE CREATOR APPS WINDOW ON ITS OWN, UNTIL 2026-09-04.
# Now the first login opens /usr/libexec/aquarius-welcome, which asks how the
# keyboard should work, then opens the creator apps window itself as its second
# step, then says goodbye. The chooser is still an ordinary app — "Aquarius
# Apps" in the app grid — it simply is not what starts at login any more.
#
# ⚠️ THIS BLOCK EXISTS BECAUSE LABWC DOES NOT READ /etc/xdg/autostart.
#
# On GNOME, a file in /etc/xdg/autostart is all it takes: GNOME reads that
# folder at login and starts everything in it. labwc reads exactly ONE file —
# this one — and nothing else. That is deliberate, and it is what keeps a dozen
# GNOME background programs out of the Aquarius session. The cost of that
# decision is this: anything that must run at login in BOTH sessions has to be
# written down twice, once as a .desktop file for GNOME and once here.
#
# So this block and AquariusOS's
# /etc/xdg/autostart/aquarius-welcome-firstrun.desktop are the same fact said in
# two languages. CHANGE ONE, CHANGE BOTH — the AquariusOS build checks that both
# exist and that they run the same command.
#
# WHAT IT OPENS. The Aquarius welcome: a short first-login flow that asks about
# the keyboard, then opens "Your creator apps" — the window that offers the
# studio apps (OBS, Kdenlive, Blender and friends) and installs the ones you
# tick — as its second step, then finishes. It is part of AquariusOS, not of
# this repository, which is why the guard below is not optional: a machine
# running this session from a clone of the shell, on plain Fedora, has no such
# program, and the session must still start.
#
# --first-run is what makes it "once": the welcome looks for
# ~/.config/aquarius/welcome-seen and returns silently if it is there. So this
# runs at every login and does nothing at all on all but the first.
#
# The ten-second wait matches the X-GNOME-Autostart-Delay in the GNOME copy. It
# is not for us — the window opens in well under a second — it is so that the
# desktop has settled and the person is looking at their computer rather than at
# a login screen when it appears.
#
# THE AquariusOS COPY IS THE AUTHORITATIVE ONE. What ships on an installed
# machine is system_files/usr/share/aquarius/labwc/autostart in the os-image
# repo; this file is what runs when the session is started from a clone. The
# lines that actually run below are kept BYTE-FOR-BYTE identical to that copy,
# so a diff between the two files shows only prose. The niri equivalent is the
# `spawn-sh-at-startup` line in ../niri/config.kdl — change that too.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [ -x /usr/libexec/aquarius-welcome ]; then
    (
        sleep 10
        /usr/libexec/aquarius-welcome --first-run
    ) >> "${AQ_LOG:-/dev/null}" 2>&1 &
fi
